  "slug": "learning-causation-2f3a",
  "summary": "New tests give spatial domains and scRNA-seq clusters formal significance, while audits expose limits in AlphaGenome, drug-response data and CRISPRi rankings.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is replacing heuristic partitioning and ranking with statistically tested outputs, plus audits of where current results fail to hold. SigDoTS applies FWER-controlled testing to spatial domains via null simulations, and ProClust merges overclustered scRNA-seq groups using log-concave modelling and projection-based tests. CellSavior adds confidence-weighted merging to ensemble cell calling. Audits point the same way: AlphaGenome CAGE and RNA-seq predictions decouple across 2,111 promoter-gene pairs, drug-response concordance across six resources is limited, CRISPRi rejuvenation rankings shift with cohort, and typical RNA-seq depths miss alternative splicing. The new element is that partitions and benchmarks now carry explicit null or error models rather than relying on defaults. Not every item addresses partitioning directly.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
